ed (parabolas meet at right angles)\"},\"showlegend\":true,\"width\":700,\"height\":700,\"plot_bgcolor\":\"white\",\"paper_bgcolor\":\"white\",\"xaxis\":{\"title\":{\"text\":\"Re\"},\"showgrid\":true,\"gridcolor\":\"#ddd\",\"linecolor\":\"#ddd\",\"linewidth\":1,\"zerolinecolor\":\"black\",\"zerolinewidth\":1},\"yaxis\":{\"title\":{\"text\":\"Im\"},\"showgrid\":true,\"scaleanchor\":\"x\",\"scaleratio\":1,\"gridcolor\":\"#ddd\",\"linecolor\":\"#ddd\",\"linewidth\":1,\"zerolinecolor\":\"black\",\"zerolinewidth\":1}}}"
            ]
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "output_type": "display_data"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Disc automorphisms\n\nThe conformal self-maps of the unit disc $\\mathbb{D} = \\{|z| < 1\\}$ are the **Möbius transformations**\n\n$$\\phi_a(z) = \\frac{z - a}{1 - \\bar a z}, \\quad |a| < 1,$$\n\npossibly composed with a rotation $z \\mapsto e^{i\\theta} z$. Under $\\phi_a$, $a$ goes to $0$, and the unit circle maps to itself.\n\nThis is a **3-real-parameter group** ($a \\in \\mathbb{D}$ + one rotation angle), the same dimension as $\\mathrm{PSU}(1,1)$.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* Check that φ_a maps the unit disc to itself: |φ_a(z)| < 1 when |z| < 1 */\nphi_a(z, a) := (z - a) / (1 - conjugate(a) * z)$\na_test : 0.3 + 0.4*%i$\ntest_pts : [0.5*exp(%i*0.3), 0.8*exp(%i*1.2), 0.95*exp(%i*5.1)]$\nfor z in test_pts do\n  print(\"|φ_a(\", float(cabs(z)), \"·e^iθ) | =\", float(cabs(phi_a(z, a_test))))$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 13,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "|φ_a( 0.49999999999999994 ·e^iθ) | = 0.380440669939273\n|φ_a( 0.8 ·e^iθ) | = 0.5540001493312654\n|φ_a( 0.95 ·e^iθ) | = 0.978433228223981"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Schwarz lemma\n\nIf $f : \\mathbb{D} \\to \\mathbb{D}$ is holomorphic with $f(0) = 0$, then $|f(z)| \\le |z|$ for all $z \\in \\mathbb{D}$, and $|f'(0)| \\le 1$. Equality at any interior point forces $f$ to be a rotation $f(z) = e^{i\\theta} z$.\n\nReading: *holomorphic self-maps of the disc fixing zero are contractions*. Combined with disc automorphisms, this characterises all such maps.\n\nDemonstration with $f(z) = z^2$ (so $f(0) = 0$ and $|z^2| \\le |z|$ on the closed disc):\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "for r in [0.2, 0.5, 0.8, 0.95] do\n  print(\"|z| = \", r, \"  |z²| = \", r^2,\n        \"  Schwarz |z²| ≤ |z|:\", r^2 <= r)$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 14,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "|z| =  0.2   |z²| =  0.04000000000000001   Schwarz |z²| ≤ |z|:\n      0.04000000000000001 <= 0.2\n|z| =  0.5   |z²| =  0.25   Schwarz |z²| ≤ |z|: 0.25 <= 0.5\n|z| =  0.8   |z²| =  0.6400000000000001   Schwarz |z²| ≤ |z|:\n      0.6400000000000001 <= 0.8\n|z| =  0.95   |z²| =  0.9025   Schwarz |z²| ≤ |z|: 0.9025 <= 0.95"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Riemann mapping theorem\n\nThe capstone result of complex analysis. **Statement**: Let $\\Omega \\subsetneq \\mathbb{C}$ be a simply connected open set ($\\Omega \\ne \\mathbb{C}$). Then there's a biholomorphism $\\Omega \\to \\mathbb{D}$.\n\nIn words: *any simply connected proper open subset of $\\mathbb{C}$ is conformally equivalent to the unit disc*. Solving a problem on an arbitrary simply connected region reduces to solving it on the disc.\n\nConcrete examples:\n- The upper half-plane $\\mathbb{H} = \\{\\mathrm{Im}\\,z > 0\\}$: $f(z) = (z - i)/(z + i)$ maps $\\mathbb{H} \\to \\mathbb{D}$.\n- The strip $\\{0 < \\mathrm{Im}\\,z < \\pi\\}$: $f(z) = \\tanh(z/2)$ maps to $\\mathbb{D}$.\n\nThese maps are unique up to a 3-parameter family of disc automorphisms.\n\nVerify the upper-half-plane to disc map:\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "f_uh(z) := (z - %i) / (z + %i)$\nhp_pts : [1.0 + 0.5*%i, 0.0 + 1.0*%i, -2.0 + 0.3*%i, 5.0 + 2.0*%i]$\nprint(\"Upper-half-plane points → disc images (|·| < 1):\")$\nfor p in hp_pts do block([img],\n  img : float(cabs(f_uh(p))),\n  print(\"  z =\", p, \"  → |f(z)| =\", img))$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 15,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "Upper-half-plane points → disc images (|·| < 1):\n  z = 0.5*%i+1.0   → |f(z)| = 0.6201736729460423\n  z = 1.0*%i   → |f(z)| = 0.0\n  z = 0.3*%i-2.0   → |f(z)| = 0.8883150852515186\n  z = 2.0*%i+5.0   → |f(z)| = 0.874474632195206"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Practical applications\n\nConformal maps are heavily used in:\n\n- **Aerodynamics** — Joukowski transformation maps a circle to an aerofoil shape; the flow around the circle (easy) gives the flow around the aerofoil (useful).\n- **Electrostatics** — solving Laplace's equation in 2D regions reduces, via conformal maps, to solving on a disc.\n- **Complex analytic dynamics** — Riemann surfaces, Möbius transformations of $\\mathbb{H}^3$, the Mandelbrot set's combinatorial structure.\n\n## What we got from Stage 3\n\nA flying tour of complex analysis:\n\n1. **Complex plane geometry** — multiplication = rotation + scaling.\n2. **Holomorphic functions and Cauchy-Riemann** — direction-independent differentiability.\n3. **Power series and analyticity** — radius of convergence governed by complex singularities.\n4. **Contour integrals and Cauchy's theorem** — closed loops integrate to zero.\n5. **Cauchy's integral formula** — interior values from boundary; analytic = $C^\\infty$.\n6. **Residues** — calculus of singularities, real integrals via the complex plane.\n7. **Conformal maps** — angle-preservation, disc automorphisms, Riemann mapping.\n\nThe arc real → $\\mathbb{R}^n$ → $\\mathbb{C}$ is now complete. Each stage builds on the last, and the rigidity gained at each step (compactness, total derivative, holomorphy) is what unlocks the next layer of techniques.\n\n## Where to go from here\n\nNatural follow-ups:\n\n- **Functional analysis** — extending to infinite-dimensional spaces; Banach and Hilbert spaces; bounded operators.\n- **Lebesgue integration and measure theory** — completing the Riemann integral; $L^p$ spaces; integration on more general measure spaces.\n- **Differential geometry / manifolds** — Stokes' theorem in its general form $\\int_M d\\omega = \\int_{\\partial M} \\omega$.\n- **Riemann surfaces** — multi-valued functions like $\\sqrt{z}$, $\\log z$ become single-valued on the right covering space.\n- **Several complex variables** — much richer than one-variable complex analysis; Hartogs's theorem, plurisubharmonic functions.\n\nThe journey is finite; the subject is not.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
}